## 4. Wrapper entrypoint

Mounted from a ConfigMap at `/opt/ws/entrypoint.sh` (or baked into the image). Adjust `L` to where the read-only tree lives.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e
L=/data/lower U=/data/overlay/upper W=/data/overlay/work M=/home/coder/workspace
mkdir -p "$L" "$U" "$W" "$M"

# PID 1 must stay in the container's original namespaces, or kubectl exec / exec probes break.
unshare -Urm sh -c '
  fuse-overlayfs -o "lowerdir=$1,upperdir=$2,workdir=$3,squash_to_root" "$4"
  exec unshare -U --map-user=1000 --map-group=1000 sh -c "$5"
' sh "$L" "$U" "$W" "$M" "$CODER_INIT_SCRIPT" &
child=$!
trap 'kill -TERM "$child"' TERM INT
wait "$child"
```

`squash_to_root` is needed when the lower tree is owned by anyone other than uid 1000 (e.g. the reconciler's root-owned `commits/<sha>/`); see [Lower tree owned by root](#lower-tree-owned-by-root-needs-squash_to_root).

What happens:

1. PID 1 (`sh`) stays in the container's namespaces and waits.
2. Child: new user + mount namespace (uid 0 inside), `fuse-overlayfs` mounts the merged view and keeps running to serve it.
3. Inner user namespace maps back to uid 1000 (mount namespace inherited) and runs the Coder agent init script.
4. Everything the agent starts (terminal, code-server, SSH sessions) inherits the overlay.

Kernel overlay alternative (no `/dev/fuse` needed, subject to Bottlerocket SELinux): replace the `fuse-overlayfs` line with `mount -t overlay overlay -o "lowerdir=$1,upperdir=$2,workdir=$3,userxattr" "$4"`.

The lab seeded the lower dir on first start (`git clone --depth 1 ... "$L"`). In the real design the lower dir is the pinned, materialized Git commit.

### 4b. Moving the base to newer commits and back (`ws-base`)

With the distributed Git storage, the lower is a pinned `commits/<sha>/` materialized by the node reconciler, and new commits keep arriving after the workspace started. `ws-base` lets the user move the workspace onto a newer commit and back again, keeping their upper-layer edits. Full script: [Appendix A](#appendix-a-ws-base). Lab: `ws-base/` (`run.sh`).

**Where it runs.** Only the **outer** nested user namespace (created by `unshare -Urm`) can unmount/mount: it owns the mount namespace. The agent, terminals and code-server run in the inner namespace and get `must be superuser to unmount`, so users cannot touch the mount directly. `ws-base serve` runs in the outer namespace as a small controller; user commands send it requests through a FIFO under `/tmp/ws-base/`.

**Entrypoint** (replaces section 4 when you use `ws-base`):

```sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e
export PATH=/opt/ws:$PATH

# PID 1 must stay in the container's original namespaces, or kubectl exec / exec probes break.
# The rebase controller lives in the outer nested namespace: it owns the mount namespace.
unshare -Urm sh -c '
  ws-base mount
  ws-base serve &
  exec unshare -U --map-user=1000 --map-group=1000 sh -c "$1"
' sh "$CODER_INIT_SCRIPT" &
child=$!
trap 'kill -TERM "$child"' TERM INT
wait "$child"
```

**Mount the export root, not one commit.** The lower must come from a volume holding the whole reconciler export root (`commits/`, `CURRENT_SHA`, `tags/`), mounted read-only at `WS_GIT_ROOT` (default `/git-backend`). The reconciler materializes into a temp dir and renames it into `commits/<sha>`, so a new commit is just a new directory on an already-mounted filesystem and shows up inside the nested namespace with no mount propagation. Mounting `commits/<sha>` alone (as in `example-workspace-pod.yaml`) makes later commits invisible.

**State** lives on the scratch PVC next to `upper/` and `work/` (`WS_SCRATCH`, default `/data/overlay`), so it survives restarts:

| File | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `.base_commit_sha` | current base; `ws-base mount` pins it on first start (`WS_BASE_REF`, default `latest`) and remounts it after a restart. It is also the parent `ws-publish` uses, so it must change with the remount (it does, atomically). |
| `.base_history` | stack of previous bases; `use` pushes, `back` pops |

**Commands** (from a Coder terminal):

| Command | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `ws-base status` | base, latest (`CURRENT_SHA`), what `back` returns to, number of upper entries |
| `ws-base list` | materialized commits and tags on this node |
| `ws-base check <ref>` | dry run: which of your changes would hide an upstream change |
| `ws-base use latest` / `use <sha-prefix>` / `use tag/<name>` | switch base; refuses if `check` finds conflicts |
| `ws-base back` | return to the previous base |
| add `--force` | switch anyway and keep your copies |

**Conflict check.** Moving the upper onto a different lower is not a Git rebase: any file in `upper/` simply shadows the new lower. If upstream changed a file you also edited (or deleted), your copy would silently hide their change, and a later `ws-publish` would revert it. Before switching, `ws-base` walks only `upper/` (cheap, no repo scan) and flags a path when the two bases differ there **and** your version differs from the new one. Convergent changes are not flagged (both deleted, or your edit equals upstream's). The check compares old base vs new base, not your file's original base, so after several switches it can be conservative (see `old.txt` below). Opaque directories stored as xattrs (kernel overlay) are not detected.

**Lab run** (kind, `restricted`, `hostUsers: false`, no added caps; a reconciler container adds commits to a shared volume after the workspace started; the workspace mounts it read-only):

```text
## start on A; user edits shared.txt, deletes old.txt, adds notes.txt
## B arrives (changes upstream.txt, adds new-in-b.txt)
$ ws-base check latest      ->  no conflicts
$ ws-base use latest        ->  rebased 6dcd4ce23d88 -> ae4f281df5a5
  VERSION=B, upstream.txt=u2, new-in-b.txt visible, shared.txt = "my edit"
## C arrives (edits shared.txt, deletes old.txt)
$ ws-base use latest        ->  refused: ... shared.txt        (old.txt converged: not flagged)
$ ws-base use latest --force->  rebased ae4f281df5a5 -> 32096c2e0eff, kept your copy of shared.txt
  .base_commit_sha = C (ws-publish parent)
$ ws-base back              ->  refused: old.txt, shared.txt
$ ws-base back --force      ->  B
$ ws-base back              ->  A
$ ws-base back              ->  no previous base
$ ws-base use tag/C --force ->  C
$ ws-base use ../../etc     ->  bad ref;  use tag/../../etc -> bad tag name
$ umount -l ~/workspace     ->  must be superuser to unmount
one fuse-overlayfs left (old ones exit once nothing holds the detached mount)
```

Things to watch:

- **Retention/GC must respect pins.** The reconciler only materializes `ACTIVE_COMMIT` and tagged commits, and a GC of `commits/<sha>/` is planned. A workspace's `.base_commit_sha` and `.base_history` entries must stay materialized, or the workspace cannot remount after a restart (`pinned base ... is not materialized on this node`) and `back` fails. Same after a reschedule to another git-worker. Options: have workspaces publish their pins (e.g. a ConfigMap/annotation the reconciler materializes and GC skips), or keep commits for N days.
- **Getting the export root into a restricted pod.** PSA `baseline` and `restricted` forbid `hostPath` volumes in the pod spec. Use a `PersistentVolume` (hostPath or `local`, with node affinity to git-workers) bound to a `ReadOnlyMany` PVC, or a CSI bind (non-FUSE) mode of git-csi. Tested: a hostPath PV + ROX PVC is admitted in `restricted` with `hostUsers: false`.
- **`squash_to_root` is mandatory** for the reconciler's root-owned tree ([section 1](#lower-tree-owned-by-root-needs-squash_to_root)); `ws-base` sets it.
- **Open handles keep the old view.** A shell whose cwd was inside the workspace, or an editor with open files, keeps seeing the old base until it re-enters the directory or reloads. Until the detached mount is released, those handles can still write into the same upper dir. Switch when the workspace is quiet.
- **Only the user's own workspace can request.** The FIFO is mode 0600 in the pod; anything running in the workspace can use it, which is the same trust level as the user. Requests are validated (hex SHA prefix that exists under `commits/`, `latest`, or `tag/<name>` without `..`).
- **Kernel overlay alternative.** If you use `mount -t overlay ... userxattr` instead of FUSE, keep `redirect_dir`, `metacopy` and `index` off (the defaults in a user namespace): changing the lower under an existing upper is only supported without them.

